SGML Scanner

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Problem Description

The goal of this project is to implement a scanner for SGML tagged documents using Java and JFlex.
The scanner preprocesses news articles by filtering irrelevant SGML content, validating proper tag nesting, and tokenizing relevant text into structured token types for processing such as indexing or classification.

The scanner reads SGML input from standard input and outputs tokens to standard output.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Features Implemented
1. SGML Tag Recognition and Validation

General OPEN-TAG and CLOSE-TAG recognition 

Case insensitive matching of tag names

Global stack maintained inside the JFlex specification to ensure the following:

	Proper tag nesting

	Detection of mismatched open and close tags

	Detection of unmatched tags at end of file

	Errors reported include:

	Tag mismatch errors

	Unmatched tags remaining on stack at EOF

2. Relevant vs Irrelevant Tag Filtering

  Relevant tags whose content is preserved:

	<Doc>

	<DocNo>

	<Text>

	<Date>

	<Headline>

	<Length>

	<P>

  Irrelevant tags filtered out (including their contents):

	<Byline>

	<Correction>

	<Correction-Date>

	<Dateline>

	<DocID>

	<Graphic>

	<Section>

	<Subject>

	<Type>

	Any unknown tags

  Paragraph tags <P> are conditionally output:

	Included only if all tags on the stack are relevant

	Filtered out if nested inside irrelevant tags

3. Token Types Supported

The scanner tokenizes text inside relevant tags into the following token types:

WORD
Alphanumeric strings such as John, abc123, compiler

NUMBER
Integers and floating point numbers with optional sign
Examples: 123, -45, 3.14
Excluded: .77, no leading digit treated as '.' and '77'

APOSTROPHIZED
Words containing apostrophes
Examples: You're, John's, O'Reilly's
Hyphenated apostrophized forms such as father-in-law's are also supported

HYPHENATED
Multi-part words connected by hyphens
Examples: data-base, father-in-law
Sequences like --- are tokenized as individual punctuation marks '-','-','-'

PUNCTUATION
Any character not contributing to the above tokens

4. Error Handling

Mismatched tags are reported and ignored to allow continued scanning

Remaining unmatched tags at EOF are reported

The scanner continues processing input after errors

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Testing

A set of test files is included in the tests directory to validate individual functionalities:

Test Cases Include:

	Apostrophized words

	Hyphenated words

	Hyphen + apostrophe combinations

	Case insensitive tag matching

	Irrelevant tag filtering

	Nested irrelevant tags

	Paragraph handling

	Tag mismatch detection

	Unmatched tags at EOF

	Word and number classification

A shell script runtests.sh executes all test files automatically.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


How to Build


From the ynoori_A1 directory navigate to the Scanner directory 
and run the program by running the following commands:

cd Scanner

make clean

make

To run the scanner on newsdata.txt file and print to terminal:

java Scanner < ../newsdata.txt

If you'd like it to print to a file

java Scanner < ../newsdata.txt > test.out

To run the full test script:

chmod +x runtests.sh
./runtests.sh

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Assumptions and Design Decisions

Apostrophized tokens are matched using JFlex’s longest possible match behavior, as described in the assignment.

Hyphens separating distinct words may be treated as punctuation unless part of a recognized hyphenated structure

Unknown tags are treated as irrelevant by default

Limitations

The scanner validates proper nesting of tags using a global stack but does not enforce a complete SGML grammar beyond matching open and close tags

Possible Improvements

Testing could be expanded to include automated comparison against reference outputs for larger document sets.

